Here, we assume the input matrix B is known, and we are only estimating the connectivity matrix A from the time series data of x and u. We construct the data matrices as
(2)
X=[x(0),x(1),…,x(T−1)],Y=[x(1),…,x(T)],U=[u(0),…,u(T−1)], \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{X}=[\boldsymbol{x}(0), \boldsymbol{x}(1), \ldots, \boldsymbol{x}(T-1)], \quad \boldsymbol{Y}=[\boldsymbol{x}(1), \ldots, \boldsymbol{x}(T)], \quad \boldsymbol{U}=[\boldsymbol{u}(0), \ldots, \boldsymbol{u}(T-1)], \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where T represents the length of the time series data. Using these matrices, the parameter matrix A can be estimated by minimizing the reconstruction error in the sense of ordinary least squares (OLS):
(3)
A^=arg⁡minA‖Y−(AX+BU)‖F2 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{\boldsymbol{A}}=\underset{\boldsymbol{A}}{\arg \min }\|\boldsymbol{Y}-(\boldsymbol{A} \boldsymbol{X}+\boldsymbol{B} \boldsymbol{U})\|_{F}^{2} \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(4)
=(Y−BU)X†, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =(\boldsymbol{Y}-\boldsymbol{B} \boldsymbol{U}) \boldsymbol{X}^{\dagger}, \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where ∥ · ∥F represents the Frobenius norm and the symbol † denotes the pseudoinverse of a matrix. This manuscript derives a theoretical framework based on this formulation, which assumes linear dynamics with the input matrix B— known. The relaxation of these assumptions—nonlinear state dynamics and the case of unknown B—is addressed in Supplementary Material B.2 and B.3.

Based on the guideline presented in the previous section, we derive analytical expressions to investigate which frequency of perturbation input will efficiently excite the dynamical modes of a system. In neuroscience, such a perturbation is analogous to tACS. Since neural dynamics possess modal frequencies, there should exist perturbation input frequencies that resonate with these modes. Such resonance leads to an amplification of x(t), which generally results in larger eigenvalues µi of the covariance matrix ΣX, reflecting increased variability along the corresponding modes. To clarify this relationship, we derive an analytical expression for x(t) to investigate how the input frequency affects its amplitude. The solution x(t) to the model in Eq. 1 can be obtained by iterating the state transition matrix. Specifically,
(10)
x(t)=Atx(0)+∑k=0t−1At−1−kBu(k)+∑k=0t−1At−1−kξ(k) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{x}(t)=\boldsymbol{A}^{t} \boldsymbol{x}(0)+\sum_{k=0}^{t-1} \boldsymbol{A}^{t-1-k} \boldsymbol{B} \boldsymbol{u}(k)+\sum_{k=0}^{t-1} \boldsymbol{A}^{t-1-k} \boldsymbol{\xi}(k) \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We assume a cosine input u(k)=∑l=1Lcos⁡(ωlk)u0\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{u}(k)=\sum_{l=1}^{L} \cos \left(\omega_{l} k\right) \boldsymbol{u}_{0}$\end{document}, where ωl are the angular frequencies. By substituting this into the second term, we can obtain the following equation by performing diagonalization of A and expressing the eigenvalues in polar form.
(11)
x(t)=xpassive(t)+xdiff(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{x}(t)=\boldsymbol{x}_{\text {passive }}(t)+\boldsymbol{x}_{\text {diff }}(t), \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(12)
xpassive(t)=Atx(0)+∑k=0t−1At−1−kξ(k) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{x}_{\text {passive }}(t)=\boldsymbol{A}^{t} \boldsymbol{x}(0)+\sum_{k=0}^{t-1} \boldsymbol{A}^{t-1-k} \boldsymbol{\xi}(k) \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(13)
xdiff(t)=∑l=1L∑d=1nRe[rdt+1eiθd(t−1)−rdtei(θdt−ωl)−rdei(ωlt−θd)+eiωl(t−1)rd2−2rdcos⁡(θd−ωl)+1vdwd⊤Bu0] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{x}_{\mathrm{diff}}(t)=\sum_{l=1}^{L} \sum_{d=1}^{n} \operatorname{Re}\left[\frac{r_{d}^{t+1} e^{i \theta_{d}(t-1)}-r_{d}^{t} e^{i\left(\theta_{d} t-\omega_{l}\right)}-r_{d} e^{i\left(\omega_{l} t-\theta_{d}\right)}+e^{i \omega_{l}(t-1)}}{r_{d}^{2}-2 r_{d} \cos \left(\theta_{d}-\omega_{l}\right)+1} \boldsymbol{v}_{d} \boldsymbol{w}_{d}^{\top} \boldsymbol{B} \boldsymbol{u}_{0}\right] \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where each eigenvalue of A is written in polar form as λd=rdeiθd\begin{document}$\lambda_{d}=r_{d} e^{i \theta_{d}}$\end{document}, with rd = λd denoting the magnitude and θd = arg(λd) denoting the argument. xpassive represents the state x that would be realized if no perturbation were applied, i.e., the state that would evolve according to Eq. 5. The vector vd denotes the right eigenvector of A associated with λd, and wd⊤\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{w}_{d}^{\top}$\end{document} denotes the corresponding left eigenvector. The detailed derivation is provided in the Supplementary Material A.2.1.

The optimal control input is derived under the condition of assuming the linear system is controllable. We consider the sequence of control inputs that minimizes the following cost function (input energy minimization) while driving the state from the initial state x0 to the terminal state xT:
(17)
M({u(t)}t=0T−1)=∑t=0T−1u(t)⊤u(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{M}\left(\{\boldsymbol{u}(t)\}_{t=0}^{T-1}\right)=\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \boldsymbol{u}(t)^{\top} \boldsymbol{u}(t), \tag{17} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
under
(18)
x(0)=x0,x(T)=xT. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{x}(0)=\boldsymbol{x}_{0}, \quad \boldsymbol{x}(T)=\boldsymbol{x}_{T} . \tag{18} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Here, the initial and terminal state conditions represent constraints on the optimization problem. This problem is expressed as follows:
(19)
u(t)∗=arg⁡minuM({u(t)}t=0T−1). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{u}(t)^{*}=\underset{\boldsymbol{u}}{\arg \min } \mathcal{M}\left(\{\boldsymbol{u}(t)\}_{t=0}^{T-1}\right) . \tag{19} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The constrained optimization problem can be solved using Lagrange multipliers. The optimal control input is given by
(20)
u(t)∗=B⊤(A⊤)T−t−1W−1(x(T)−ATx(0)), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{u}(t)^{*}=\boldsymbol{B}^{\top}\left(\boldsymbol{A}^{\top}\right)^{T-t-1} \boldsymbol{W}^{-1}\left(\boldsymbol{x}(T)-\boldsymbol{A}^{T} \boldsymbol{x}(0)\right), \tag{20} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where W is the controllability Gramian, defined as:
(21)
W=∑t=0T−1AtBB⊤(A⊤)t. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{W}=\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \boldsymbol{A}^{t} \boldsymbol{B} \boldsymbol{B}^{\top}\left(\boldsymbol{A}^{\top}\right)^{t} . \tag{21} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The controllability Gramian plays a pivotal role in determining the optimal control and control input cost. It has been used to identify the functional roles of individual brain regions [42, 47].
